From: Replit task #2263
Subject: [PATCH] Guard DOM-only color helpers so pdf.js can run inside a Worker

pdf.js v5 is otherwise worker-friendly: getDocument / getPage / page.render
all already work with an OffscreenCanvas inside a dedicated module worker.
The one remaining footgun is `ColorManager.convert`, which reaches
`window.matchMedia("(forced-colors: active)")` with no environment guard.
In a worker `window` is undefined and the call throws
`ReferenceError: window is not defined`, breaking the very first
page.render() call.

`ColorScheme.isDarkMode` already uses optional chaining
(`window?.matchMedia?.(...)`) and is safe; only `ColorManager.convert`
needs to be brought up to the same standard. We also add a tiny helper
so future DOM-only forced-colors code paths can opt in by calling it
instead of inlining the check (and re-introducing the same footgun).

Without the guard, every consumer that runs pdfjs-dist inside a worker
has to ship a `window` / `matchMedia` polyfill on the worker global.
With it, the worker can `import "pdfjs-dist"` directly and behave the
same as the main-thread bundle.

Target file in upstream source tree:
  src/display/editor/tools.js  (the file that defines ColorManager)

Indices and surrounding lines below are illustrative — please regenerate
against the current upstream HEAD before posting; the change itself is
the four-line guard inside `convert()` and the matching helper above it.

---
diff --git a/src/display/editor/tools.js b/src/display/editor/tools.js
--- a/src/display/editor/tools.js
+++ b/src/display/editor/tools.js
@@
+// Worker-safe check for the OS "forced colors" / high-contrast mode.
+// Returns false (the default render path) when `window` is unavailable
+// — e.g. when pdf.js runs inside a dedicated Web Worker — instead of
+// throwing `ReferenceError: window is not defined`. Mirrors the
+// optional-chaining style already used by `ColorScheme.isDarkMode`.
+function isForcedColorsActive() {
+  return (
+    typeof window !== "undefined" &&
+    !!window.matchMedia?.("(forced-colors: active)").matches
+  );
+}
+
 class ColorManager {
   static _colorsMapping = new Map([
     ["CanvasText", [0, 0, 0]],
     ["Canvas", [255, 255, 255]],
   ]);

   get _colors() {
     const colors = new Map([
       ["CanvasText", null],
       ["Canvas", null],
     ]);
     getColorValues(colors);
     return shadow(this, "_colors", colors);
   }

   convert(color) {
     const rgb = getRGB(color);
-    if (!window.matchMedia("(forced-colors: active)").matches) {
+    if (!isForcedColorsActive()) {
       return rgb;
     }
     for (const [name, RGB] of this._colors) {
       if (RGB.every((x, i) => x === rgb[i])) {
         return ColorManager._colorsMapping.get(name);
       }
     }
     return rgb;
   }
--

Test plan for upstream reviewers
================================
1. Run the existing test suite: `gulp lint && gulp unittest && gulp browsertest`
   — none of the high-contrast / forced-colors regression tests change
   behaviour because `window` is always defined in the browser harnesses.
2. Add a node-side smoke test that imports `tools.js` after deleting
   `globalThis.window` and asserts `new ColorManager().convert("#abc")`
   returns the rgb triple instead of throwing. (This is the exact
   failure mode reproduced in worker contexts.)
3. Confirm that with this patch a dedicated module worker can call
   `getDocument(...).promise` followed by `page.render({ canvas, ... })`
   without first installing a `window` shim. The downstream consumer
   that motivated this patch (Replit, task #2263) maintains a worker
   harness that asserts exactly this and will drop its shim once the
   guard is in place.
